chapters = {
'Hollywood': [
c(0,'Greystone: oil wealth becomes a film set','Greystone Mansion',"Welcome to Hollywood's houses, movie palaces and hills. Our first stop is Greystone Mansion. The Doheny family moved here in 1928, into a home built from a fortune made in oil. Its stone walls later found a second career as a filming location. That is our theme today: how Los Angeles turns real buildings into imagined worlds. Settle into your rhythm. Short stories will reward your progress, with quiet stretches in between.",G),
c(2,'Behind the mansion: a public park','Greystone Mansion',"A little more of Greystone's story as we leave Beverly Hills. The city bought the estate in 1965, and it was dedicated as a public park in 1971. Beneath that glamorous setting is something unexpectedly practical: a major city reservoir. A film location, a former family estate and part of a water system can occupy the same hillside. Keep that double life in mind as we approach a very different sort of house.",G),
c(3.6,'Schindler House: an experiment in living','Schindler House',"Our route reaches the neighbourhood of the Schindler House, completed in 1922. Rudolph Schindler questioned the usual boundary between indoors and outdoors, and between private life and shared space. His wife Pauline helped make the house a meeting place for artists, writers and musicians. Compared with Greystone's display of wealth, this is a different ambition: architecture as a proposal for everyday life. What would a house designed around your own creative work look like?",S),
c(5.8,'A house that kept asking questions','Schindler House',"Between landmarks, a final thought about Schindler's experiment. The house is now home to the MAK Center, where exhibitions and performances continue to explore art and architecture. Preservation here is more than keeping an old building standing. It also means continuing the conversations that happened inside it. You have earned another chapter. Keep the pedals turning; next we move from private experiments to Hollywood's very public spectacle.",S),
c(8.1,'The Chinese Theatre: the entrance is a show','Grauman\'s Chinese Theatre',"We are near the Chinese Theatre on Hollywood Boulevard, which opened in 1927. Think of the cinema visit as beginning before the film: the approach, the facade and the expectation of entering somewhere extraordinary. Movie palaces made that arrival part of the entertainment. After two houses with very different ideas of domestic life, this building offers a third kind of architecture: a place built around a shared audience and the promise of a story.",C),
c(10.4,'From audience to imagination','Grauman\'s Chinese Theatre',"As the theatre falls behind us, compare the buildings we have met. Greystone became a setting for other people's stories. Schindler's house encouraged people to invent new ways of living. A movie palace gathered an audience ready to believe in another world. This connection is an interpretation, rather than a hidden historical fact: buildings can shape our expectations before anything happens inside. Your next landmark takes that idea into a garden on a hill.",C),
c(12.6,'Aline Barnsdall\'s unfinished dream','Hollyhock House',"On the way toward Hollyhock House, meet the woman behind it. Aline Barnsdall was immersed in experimental theatre. She imagined a hilltop community of theatres, shops and residences, and commissioned Frank Lloyd Wright to help create it. Only part of that larger scheme was built. The house that survives is therefore also evidence of an unfinished ambition. A landmark can tell us as much about what never happened as about what did.",H),
c(14.9,'Hollyhock House: half house, half garden','Hollyhock House',"Near Barnsdall Art Park stands Hollyhock House. Barnsdall wanted a home connected to its garden; terraces and outdoor passages carry that wish into the design. Stylised versions of her favourite flower, the hollyhock, became a recurring decoration. The city accepted the property as an art park in 1927. An intensely personal commission became a public place. We have reached the last part of our journey, with the observatory still ahead.",H),
c(17.2,'A telescope for everyone','Griffith Observatory',"Our final destination changes the meaning of stars. Griffith Observatory opened in 1935, following Griffith J. Griffith's vision of a public observatory. Its purpose was to let ordinary visitors encounter astronomy. After houses shaped by private fortunes and a theatre shaped by entertainment, this is a different invitation: shared access to scientific wonder. The next story waits near the finish. Keep a comfortable rhythm as we work toward the last landmark.",O),
c(19.4,'Griffith: a different kind of Hollywood ending','Griffith Observatory',"You have reached the final story near Griffith Observatory. A major renovation between 2002 and 2006 expanded the institution while carrying its public purpose forward. Our route has connected oil wealth, artistic experiment, cinema and the sky. Each place offered a different answer to what a building can do for people. Now the last stretch belongs to you. Enjoy finishing a ride that has travelled through ideas as well as streets.",O),
],
'Paris': [
c(0,'Bastille: the column marks another revolution','Place de la Bastille',"Welcome to a ride through the changing meanings of Paris. We begin at the Bastille, associated with the storming of the fortress in 1789. But the July Column commemorates the revolution of 1830. The square holds more than one uprising in its memory. That is our thread today: a building, a name or a monument can survive while its meaning changes. Settle into your pace. Each landmark brings a short story, with quiet riding in between.",B),
c(2.4,'The Conciergerie: from power to imprisonment','Conciergerie',"Near the Conciergerie, the story becomes personal. Marie Antoinette was transferred here in August 1793 and spent seventy-six days imprisoned before her trial and execution. Accounts of her confinement accumulated rumours and legends; the monument's own history warns that the details are not always secure. It is worth separating what a place documents from what later generations wanted it to mean. These walls held a former queen after the monarchy had fallen.",Q),
c(4.9,'Concorde: a hopeful name over a difficult past','Place de la Concorde',"We are approaching Place de la Concorde. During the Revolution this was Place de la Revolution, a site of public executions. The name Concorde, adopted in 1795, offered a different message: harmony after conflict. Names do work in a city. They can mark a change, express an aspiration or soften a difficult memory. As you pedal through this chapter, think of another familiar place whose name tells only part of its history.",D),
c(7.3,'The Arc: thirty years to finish an idea','Arc de Triomphe',"Near the Arc de Triomphe, revolutionary history gives way to a long construction story. The project began under Napoleon in 1806 and was completed in 1836, after several changes of regime. Each government had to decide what to do with a monument begun by an earlier ruler. The completed arch looks like a single statement in stone. Its construction was anything but a single uninterrupted act. You have reached another landmark; let the next stretch unfold at your own pace.",A),
c(9.1,'The wedding arch made from canvas','Arc de Triomphe',"A surprising detail from the arch we have just left: for Napoleon's wedding procession in 1810, the real structure was unfinished. A full-size temporary version was made from frames and painted canvas. Imagine a monument briefly existing as scenery before it existed in stone. This is historical context for the stretch between landmarks, rather than another arch beside the route. Our next chapters turn from the memory of rulers toward the people a nation chooses to honour.",A),
c(11,'A king\'s promise, a national monument','Panthéon',"As we cross toward the Left Bank, the Pantheon's origin prepares our next stop. Louis the Fifteenth promised a new church after recovering from illness. The project was financed in part through a royal lottery, and the foundation stone was laid in 1764. Construction lasted long enough for the political world around it to change. A building planned as an act of royal devotion would soon acquire a very different public role. Keep pedalling; its next chapter is ahead.",P),
c(13.8,'The Panthéon: who gets remembered?','Panthéon',"Near the Pantheon, we reach a building repeatedly reassigned between church and secular monument. Voltaire and Rousseau were honoured here during the revolutionary period. Later governments changed its role again. Victor Hugo's funeral in 1885 helped establish its lasting function as a national place of remembrance. The question is bigger than architecture: who should speak for a nation's history, and who decides? Your progress has brought you to the final landmark of our loop.",P),
c(15.4,'Back to Bastille: reading the layers','Place de la Bastille',"As we approach the end of our Paris loop, return in your mind to the Bastille. The fortress was demolished, but its name still anchors the city. A later column stands over the canal, carrying another layer of revolutionary memory. We have met a prison, a renamed square, an arch and a former church. None has kept just one meaning. Carry that way of looking into your next real city walk. Enjoy the last stretch; you have explored every story.",B),
],
'Rome': [
c(0,'Rome: a city that moved people','Colosseum',"Welcome to Rome. We begin near the Colosseum, then ride outward toward the Appian Way before returning. Our thread is movement: crowds gathering, water reaching the baths, travellers leaving the city, and memories carried along a road. These chapters connect public landmarks with the lives that gave them purpose. This is an indoor interpretation of the route, with quiet stretches between stories. Find your rhythm; the next chapter waits as we leave the ancient centre.",'https://www.turismoroma.it/en/places/colosseum'),
c(1.5,'Circus Maximus: sharing a spectacle','Circus Maximus',"Our route passes near the Circus Maximus, Rome's great chariot-racing ground. Compare the shape of a racing circuit with the amphitheatre we have left behind. Different buildings organised different spectacles, but both turned a crowd into part of the event. Looking at an ancient site as a place people used helps it feel less remote. Instead of seeing only a monument, consider the anticipation of arriving, finding a place and waiting for the competition to begin.",'https://www.turismoroma.it/en/places/circus-maximus'),
c(3.4,'Caracalla: much more than a bath','Baths of Caracalla',"Near the Baths of Caracalla, everyday life takes on an imperial scale. The complex was built between the years 212 and 216. Visitors came for bathing and exercise, but also for walking and study. A dedicated branch of an aqueduct supplied its water. The surviving walls are only part of the picture: coloured marble, mosaics and statues once enriched the spaces. Imagine the effort required to make this much public leisure possible.",T),
c(5.5,'The work beneath the water','Baths of Caracalla',"As we leave the baths behind, their less visible story deserves a chapter. Service spaces spread below the complex, supporting what happened above. Architecture that appears effortless to a visitor often depends on a great deal of hidden work. Here, a water supply and a network of working spaces sustained the public experience. That is our connection to the next landmark: Rome's impressive surfaces were supported by systems that kept people and resources moving.",T),
c(7.6,'The Appian Way: a road with a name','Appian Way',"We reach the corridor of the Appian Way, begun in 312 before the common era. It took its name from Appius Claudius, the official responsible for it, rather than simply naming its destination. Its builders aimed for a direct line, tackling obstacles with engineering. The road first connected Rome with Capua and was later extended toward Brindisi. A journey that feels local on our map belonged to a much larger network of movement and power.",R),
c(9.5,'Stone under wheels','Appian Way',"On this outward stretch, consider how a road becomes durable. Sections of the ancient Appian Way retain large volcanic paving stones. The road supported traffic in both directions, with walking space at its sides. We are following an indoor route, so the trainer cannot reproduce every surface beneath a Roman traveller's feet. The photograph offers another clue: a road is a designed object, with choices about materials, width and the different people using it.",R),
c(11.5,'Beyond the centre: villas and tombs','Villa of the Quintilii',"Our route reaches its outer section near the Villa of the Quintilii. Rome's tourism itinerary places villas, tombs and other monuments along the Appian landscape. A road carries more than traffic: the places beside it can display wealth, preserve names and shape how travellers encounter a city. Let that be the question for this quieter section. What do the buildings along your own daily route tell a visitor about the place where you live?",R),
c(14,'The return: a road outlives its builders','Appian Way',"We are on the return half of the ride. The Appian Way's uses changed long after its ancient construction. Medieval pilgrims travelled it because of the catacombs and the onward journey available from Brindisi. Later efforts helped recover the ancient route as a place to explore. A road can survive through changing purposes rather than remaining frozen in one period. You have earned another chapter. Keep a comfortable rhythm as we turn our attention back toward the city.",R),
c(16.5,'A gate is a decision about a city','Porta San Sebastiano',"The Appian corridor leads toward Porta San Sebastiano, formerly called Porta Appia. Rome's Museum of the Walls occupies the gate and tells the history of the fortifications. A road invites movement while a wall controls it; a gate is where those two ideas meet. This chapter introduces the city boundary as historical context for our return, without assuming every ancient structure is visible from the route. The last stories will bring our journey back to its beginning.",R),
c(19,'Rome beyond the postcard','Baths of Caracalla',"As we return toward central Rome, think back to the baths. Their decoration once included coloured stone, mosaics and large sculptural groups. Ruins often present us with a quieter, more uniform appearance than the spaces their users knew. Reconstructing that difference in your mind changes the experience of a familiar landmark. You have followed roads, water and public gathering places. One final chapter remains near the finish, bringing those threads back together.",T),
c(21.3,'The final chapter: infrastructure and imagination','Colosseum',"You are close to the end of our Rome loop. We began with a monumental gathering place and followed the city's connections outward. The baths needed water and working spaces; the Appian Way carried journeys far beyond the centre. Reading a city through those connections gives its famous buildings a human scale. Take that thought into the final stretch. Your own steady movement has linked every chapter of this ride, one landmark and one turn of the pedals at a time.",'https://www.turismoroma.it/en/places/colosseum'),
],
'Whitechapel': [
c(0,'Whitechapel: start with the people','Whitechapel',"Welcome to a thoughtful ride through the East End. In 1888, five women's deaths became linked to the unidentified killer called Jack the Ripper. We will keep the account non-graphic and focus on lives, evidence and how a story becomes a legend. The neighbourhood was a place where people worked and made homes, not simply a crime scene. Settle into your rhythm. Short chapters will accompany your progress around the loop.",L),
c(1.5,'Spitalfields: a neighbourhood, not a backdrop','Spitalfields',"Our route takes us through the wider Whitechapel and Spitalfields area. Nineteenth-century Whitechapel was home to a large Jewish community, and tailoring was an important source of work. London Museum describes crowded housing and uncertain earnings alongside the capital's immense wealth. That wider setting matters. A story about a place becomes more useful when it includes the ordinary lives that sensational headlines leave out.",L),
c(3.1,'Five names to carry forward','Whitechapel',"At this chapter, pause over the names: Mary Ann Nichols, Annie Chapman, Elizabeth Stride, Catherine Eddowes and Mary Jane Kelly. None was born in Whitechapel. Their lives reached beyond the neighbourhood and beyond the events that made them famous. Acknowledging them as people changes the emphasis of the ride. Keep those names with you through the next quiet stretch.",L),
c(5.1,'A letter is evidence, but of what?','Whitechapel',"Between landmarks, an object from the National Archives offers a useful question. Police files contain hundreds of letters claiming a connection with the Whitechapel murderer, including hoaxes. The existence of a dramatic letter does not prove that its author committed a crime. It may tell us something different about the attention the case attracted. As you pedal onward, consider the distinction between an authentic historical object and a truthful account.",N),
c(7.4,'The news travels beyond London','Whitechapel',"Another chapter in the making of the legend: news of the murders travelled internationally. The transatlantic telegraph connection helped reports reach North America. A local event could become a shared story far beyond the streets where it happened. That observation connects Victorian London with a question that still matters: when a story spreads quickly, how do we keep curiosity from outrunning evidence?",L),
c(9.4,'Uncertainty is part of the history','Whitechapel',"The investigation never produced a charge against the killer. Later theories cannot make that uncertainty disappear. For this ride, leaving the case unresolved is an honest part of understanding it. The point is not to reward a guess with the appearance of certainty. Your next chapter is the closing one, near Mitre Square. Keep the pedals moving while we make room for a quieter kind of historical curiosity.",L),
c(11.8,'Mitre Square: remembering beyond the legend','Mitre Square',"Near Mitre Square, we reach our final chapter and remember Catherine Eddowes. We have followed a neighbourhood, five names and the difference between a claim and evidence. A considerate historical journey can leave questions open while paying attention to the people involved. As you finish the loop, carry that approach forward: curiosity with care, and room for lives beyond the headlines. Thank you for riding this reflective route.",L),
],
'Alpe': [
c(0,'The Alpe: one chapter at a time',"Le Bourg-d'Oisans","Welcome to the climb from Bourg d'Oisans toward Alpe d'Huez. The famous road is known for its twenty-one bends, but today your challenge is personal. We will break the ascent into short story milestones, with quiet riding in between. Our indoor route is an interpretation of the approach, rather than an official race timing course. Begin at a pace you can settle into. Your first reward is already here: the story of a road that became a sporting landmark.",E),
c(1.4,'Why the bends matter',"Alpe d'Huez","The climb's numbered bends turn a long ascent into visible steps. The resort describes twenty-one signs marking progress up the road. That is a useful idea to borrow indoors: a distant finish becomes a sequence of smaller arrivals. Our chapter markers serve the same purpose without pretending to match every official bend. Keep your effort comfortable enough to enjoy the journey. The next story is another small destination, rather than the whole mountain at once.",E),
c(2.8,'1952: a new summit finish',"Alpe d'Huez","In 1952, the Tour de France brought its riders to Alpe d'Huez for the first time. Fausto Coppi won the stage and took the yellow jersey on his way to winning that year's Tour. This was the beginning of the climb's racing story, not the end of its development. A road we now treat as familiar was once an experiment for race organisers. You have reached another chapter of your own first, or latest, encounter with it.",F),
c(4.2,'A famous road is not famous overnight',"La Garde, Isère","The Alpe's first Tour appearance did not immediately make it a regular fixture. The race returned in 1976, more than two decades after Coppi's victory. Reputation can feel inevitable when we look backward, but it often develops through separate moments. As our route works toward La Garde, let that thought soften the pressure of the famous name. This is a road with a history, and your ride can be worthwhile without resembling a professional race.",W),
c(5.6,'The road as a grandstand',"Alpe d'Huez","At a hairpin, a road doubles back on itself. That geometry helps explain why a winding ascent is so memorable on a map: the distance travelled and the straight line to the finish tell different stories. The Alpe's numbered bends give that shape a vocabulary. This is a reading of the route rather than a claim about a particular race crowd beside you. Your next chapter is waiting further up; enjoy making one more section your own.",E),
c(7,'What a climbing legend leaves out',"Alpe d'Huez","When we remember Coppi's first victory here, it is easy to compress an entire stage into its final climb. The Tour's account begins the day in Lausanne, far from the Alpe. That wider journey matters: a memorable finish is supported by many less celebrated kilometres. Our indoor ride gives you the ascent on its own. Take a moment to recognise the distance you have already covered, including the stretches that passed without a story.",F),
c(8.4,'Halfway is its own achievement',"Alpe d'Huez","You have reached the middle chapters of this route. The resort's published figures describe an ascent of roughly fourteen kilometres gaining more than a thousand metres; our route and its elevation model use their own endpoints. Small differences between route lengths do not erase the scale of the challenge. Let the next marker be your immediate goal. The story of a climb is built from accumulated progress, and you are adding to yours now.",E),
c(9.8,'Huez: a village before the resort','Huez',"As we work toward Huez, the village offers a history older than cycle racing. The municipality describes a medieval community whose surviving traces include a restored tower. For centuries, farming, travelling trade and small crafts shaped life here. A famous sporting name can hide that longer everyday story. Following the place names restores some of the geography: this is a mountain where people lived and worked long before it became a destination for visiting riders.",V),
c(11.2,'The Dutch chapter in a mountain story',"Alpe d'Huez","The climb's list of winners has a striking run of Dutch names. Joop Zoetemelk won here in 1976 and 1979. Hennie Kuiper and Peter Winnen also each recorded two victories, followed by Steven Rooks and Gert-Jan Theunisse in the late nineteen-eighties. A mountain in France became part of many countries' sporting memories. Think of that shared history as company for this stretch, while keeping your own effort and target entirely your own.",W),
c(12.6,'Brandes: silver above the valley','Huez',"Beyond our route, on the upper plateau, lies the medieval mining site of Brandes. A settlement grew there to exploit silver-bearing lead at more than eighteen hundred metres above sea level. Its remains include homes and working areas, evidence of a permanent community in a demanding location. Flooding brought the mine to an end in the thirteen-thirties, and the village emptied. The mountain's history includes extraction and labour as well as the leisure activities that draw visitors today.",M),
c(14,'Winter sport transforms the mountain',"Alpe d'Huez","The approaching resort tells another chapter of change. The municipality's history describes skiing as a turning point in the twentieth century. Lifts, hotels, roads and sporting facilities transformed an agricultural mountain community into an international tourism destination. The road you are following belongs to that broader story, not only to the Tour. One final chapter remains. Keep a comfortable rhythm as the destination becomes closer and the different histories of the mountain come together.",V),
c(15.1,'Your own version of the Alpe',"Alpe d'Huez","You have unlocked the final chapter. We followed the approach from Bourg d'Oisans, met the Alpe's racing origins and used small milestones to make a long climb feel closer. The famous road supplies a setting, but the time and effort of this session belong to you. Enjoy the remaining distance without needing a dramatic last push. When the ride ends, you will have linked every chapter with your own movement, one quiet stretch and one reward at a time.",E),
]}

routes=json.loads(FILE.read_text())
for route in routes:
    key=next(key for key in chapters if route['name'].startswith(key))
    stops=[]
    for item in chapters[key]:
        point=min(route['points'],key=lambda p:abs(p['distanceFromStart']-item['distanceKm']))
        sid=str(uuid.uuid5(uuid.NAMESPACE_URL,route['id']+'/'+item['title']));sid=sid[:14]+'4'+sid[15:]
        stops.append(dict(item,id=sid,lat=point['lat'],lng=point['lng']))
    revision=hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(stops,sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()
    gid=str(uuid.uuid5(uuid.NAMESPACE_URL,route['id']+'/editorial/'+revision));gid=gid[:14]+'4'+gid[15:]
    old={stop['id']:stop for stop in route['guide']['stops']}
    for stop in stops:
        cached=old.get(stop['id'],{})
        if cached.get('text')==stop['text'] and cached.get('audioSeconds'):
            stop['audioSeconds']=cached['audioSeconds']
    route['guide'].update(id=gid,stops=stops)
    # The source authoring file is the editorial layer after geometry generation.
    print(route['name'],len(stops),'stories',sum(len(s['text'].split()) for s in stops),'words')
FILE.write_text(json.dumps(routes,ensure_ascii=False,separators=(',',':')))
